---
/**
 * The body in the sky: the moon at night, the sun by day.
 *
 * One object with two faces rather than two features. Both are in the markup and
 * the scheme picks which renders, via `--mg-sky-night` / `--mg-sky-day` in
 * global.css — the one thing the palette genuinely cannot express, because no
 * colour turns a crescent into a disc. It is `display`, not `opacity`: the
 * hidden one should not be composited at all, and at 0 opacity it still would.
 *
 * POSITIONED IN CSS, NOT IN THE SVG. Everything else in the sky is placed with
 * percentage coordinates inside one full-bleed SVG, but this needs to MOVE
 * between layouts — on a phone the plate fills the upper left, so the moon has
 * to go somewhere else entirely — and a `<circle>`'s cx/cy are attributes that a
 * media query cannot reach. Wrapping it in a positioned box makes the placement
 * an ordinary two-line CSS override, and lets the drawing below use plain user
 * units with no percentages in it at all.
 *
 * THE MOON IS GIBBOUS, cut by a mask rather than drawn as a lune: the terminator
 * on a real moon is the edge of a circle seen at an angle, so subtracting an
 * offset circle of the same radius gives exactly the right curve for free, and
 * the phase is then one number — how far the two centres are apart.
 *
 * The maria are inside the mask, so they are clipped by the terminator like
 * anything else on the surface. Drawn over it they would float on the dark limb
 * and read as holes.
 *
 * The halo stays a full circle behind both faces. It is atmosphere, not surface,
 * so it does not take the phase.
 */
---

<div class="mg-celestial" aria-hidden="true">
  <svg
    class="mg-celestial__svg"
    viewBox="0 0 120 120"
    role="presentation"
    focusable="false"
  >
    <defs>
      {/* Both halos fade to nothing well inside their own circle: a glow that is
          still carrying weight at its edge draws a visible rim, which is the one
          thing a glow must not do. */}
      <radialGradient class="mg-halo-moon" id="mg-halo-moon">
        <stop offset="0.4" stop-opacity="0.5" />
        <stop offset="0.68" stop-opacity="0.14" />
        <stop offset="1" stop-opacity="0" />
      </radialGradient>

      <radialGradient class="mg-halo-sun" id="mg-halo-sun">
        <stop offset="0.34" stop-opacity="0.62" />
        <stop offset="0.6" stop-opacity="0.2" />
        <stop offset="1" stop-opacity="0" />
      </radialGradient>

      {/* Phase. The dark circle is the same radius as the disc and offset up and
          to the left; how far apart their centres sit IS the phase, and at 46
          against a diameter of 68 that is a waxing gibbous. Move it toward the
          disc for a crescent, away for a full moon. */}
      <mask id="mg-moon-phase">
        <circle cx="60" cy="60" r="34" fill="#fff" />
        <circle cx="18" cy="41" r="34" fill="#000" />
      </mask>
    </defs>

    <g class="mg-moon">
      <circle class="mg-moon__halo" cx="60" cy="60" r="58" />
      <g mask="url(#mg-moon-phase)">
        <circle class="mg-moon__disc" cx="60" cy="60" r="34" />
        {/* Maria. Large, soft and few — at this size the moon is about 60px
            across on screen, and any more detail than three patches turns into
            speckle rather than reading as markings. */}
        <ellipse class="mg-moon__mare" cx="70" cy="48" rx="13" ry="10" />
        <ellipse class="mg-moon__mare" cx="55" cy="74" rx="10" ry="8" />
        <ellipse class="mg-moon__mare" cx="80" cy="72" rx="7" ry="6" />
      </g>
    </g>

    <g class="mg-sun">
      <circle class="mg-sun__halo" cx="60" cy="60" r="58" />
      <circle class="mg-sun__disc" cx="60" cy="60" r="27" />
    </g>
  </svg>
</div>
